WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.269
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C one, Book four.

2
00:00:05.569 --> 00:00:09.013
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.313 --> 00:00:16.377
Unit seventeen: International Business. Today you will state conditions for success abroad with when + clause.

4
00:00:16.677 --> 00:00:19.295
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:23.547
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.383 --> 00:00:25.605
Globalization.

7
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:30.578
Globalization means the growing connection of economies and cultures worldwide.

8
00:00:31.183 --> 00:00:34.634
Globalization brought Cambodian garments to European shops.

9
00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.657
Your turn. Say: Globalization.

10
00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:42.803
Globalization.

11
00:00:43.550 --> 00:00:45.422
Cross-border trade.

12
00:00:46.417 --> 00:00:49.995
Cross-border trade means buying and selling goods between countries.

13
00:00:50.583 --> 00:00:54.104
Cross-border trade with Vietnam rose sharply last year.

14
00:00:54.717 --> 00:00:58.321
Your turn. Say: Cross-border trade.

15
00:01:01.167 --> 00:01:03.073
Cross-border trade.

16
00:01:03.817 --> 00:01:05.294
Cultural competence.

17
00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:10.895
Cultural competence means the ability to work effectively with people from other cultures.

18
00:01:11.483 --> 00:01:14.911
Cultural competence helped the team close the Japanese deal.

19
00:01:15.517 --> 00:01:18.494
Your turn. Say: Cultural competence.

20
00:01:21.333 --> 00:01:22.950
Cultural competence.

21
00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.288
Market entry strategy.

22
00:01:26.283 --> 00:01:30.872
Market entry strategy means a plan for how a firm will start selling in a new country.

23
00:01:31.483 --> 00:01:34.992
Their market entry strategy was a joint venture with a local partner.

24
00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:38.909
Your turn. Say: Market entry strategy.

25
00:01:41.767 --> 00:01:44.138
Market entry strategy.

26
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:45.745
Tariff.

27
00:01:46.750 --> 00:01:49.841
Tariff means a tax on imported or exported goods.

28
00:01:50.450 --> 00:01:53.680
New tariffs made imported cars far more expensive.

29
00:01:54.283 --> 00:01:56.494
Your turn. Say: Tariff.

30
00:01:59.333 --> 00:02:00.311
Tariff.

31
00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.365
Localization.

32
00:02:03.350 --> 00:02:08.020
Localization means adapting a product to a specific country's language and culture.

33
00:02:08.617 --> 00:02:12.439
Localization included Khmer menus and local payment options.

34
00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:16.039
Your turn. Say: Localization.

35
00:02:18.900 --> 00:02:20.516
Localization.

36
00:02:21.250 --> 00:02:22.855
Foreign direct investment.

37
00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:28.891
Foreign direct investment means investment by a company in business operations in another country.

38
00:02:29.483 --> 00:02:32.853
Foreign direct investment created thousands of factory jobs.

39
00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:36.938
Your turn. Say: Foreign direct investment.

40
00:02:39.800 --> 00:02:41.788
Foreign direct investment.

41
00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:44.155
Global supply chain.

42
00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:49.530
Global supply chain means a production network that spans several countries.

43
00:02:50.117 --> 00:02:53.637
A phone's global supply chain may involve twenty countries.

44
00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:57.494
Your turn. Say: Global supply chain.

45
00:03:00.333 --> 00:03:02.066
Global supply chain.

46
00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:06.332
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:03:07.183 --> 00:03:13.458
Visal, a Korean food chain opened five outlets in Phnom Penh and closed three within a year. What went wrong?

48
00:03:14.550 --> 00:03:23.193
They copied their Seoul menu exactly. Cultural competence matters most when entering a new market, and they ignored local tastes.

49
00:03:24.283 --> 00:03:26.318
Surely good food sells everywhere?

50
00:03:27.417 --> 00:03:37.488
Not always. When customers expect rice with every meal and shared dishes, individual portions without rice feel strange and expensive.

51
00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:39.921
How could they recover?

52
00:03:41.017 --> 00:03:50.403
By localising. When they partnered with a Cambodian chef and adjusted portions, sales in the remaining outlets rose by 40%.

53
00:03:51.567 --> 00:03:53.206
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:57.257
Grammar focus: When plus clause for conditions.

55
00:03:57.557 --> 00:03:58.802
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:59.517 --> 00:04:02.770
Cultural competence matters most when entering a new market.

57
00:04:03.683 --> 00:04:06.867
Firms succeed abroad when they adapt to local customs.

58
00:04:07.783 --> 00:04:11.002
When tariffs rise, exporters look for new markets.

59
00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:22.224
For general truths and future conditions, use the present tense after 'when': When we enter Japan, we will adapt (not 'when we will enter'). 'When + -ing' is a concise formal option.

60
00:04:22.983 --> 00:04:26.971
Be careful! Don't say: When we will enter Japan, we'll adapt.

61
00:04:28.933 --> 00:04:31.479
Say: When we enter Japan, we'll adapt.

62
00:04:31.779 --> 00:04:33.650
Present tense after 'when' (future).

63
00:04:34.467 --> 00:04:37.491
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.265
Thinking Global, Acting Local.

65
00:04:40.565 --> 00:04:47.742
International business offers access to larger markets, cheaper inputs and new ideas, but it also multiplies complexity.

66
00:04:48.733 --> 00:04:55.783
When a company crosses a border, it faces different laws, currencies, tariffs and, above all, different customer expectations.

67
00:04:56.767 --> 00:05:05.581
Firms choose among market entry strategies such as exporting, licensing, joint ventures or foreign direct investment, each carrying different levels of risk and control.

68
00:05:06.567 --> 00:05:11.829
Many failures abroad stem not from poor products but from a lack of cultural competence.

69
00:05:12.833 --> 00:05:17.375
Companies tend to thrive when they combine global efficiency with genuine localization.

70
00:05:18.467 --> 00:05:22.573
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:23.417 --> 00:05:27.134
When the company blank Thailand next year, it will hire locally.

72
00:05:27.434 --> 00:05:30.716
A, will enter. B, enters. C, entered.

73
00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.092
The answer is B: enters.

74
00:05:37.250 --> 00:05:39.575
A tax on imported goods is a blank.

75
00:05:39.875 --> 00:05:44.828
A, tariff. B, localization. C, globalization.

76
00:05:48.367 --> 00:05:50.006
The answer is A: tariff.

77
00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.415
Adapting a product to a country's language and culture is blank.

78
00:05:54.715 --> 00:05:59.994
A, foreign direct investment. B, cross-border trade. C, localization.

79
00:06:03.533 --> 00:06:06.171
The answer is C: localization.

80
00:06:07.317 --> 00:06:08.991
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:06:09.291 --> 00:06:16.288
A, When we will arrive, we'll call you.. B, When we arrive, we'll call you.. C, When we arrived, we'll call you..

82
00:06:19.833 --> 00:06:22.704
The answer is B: When we arrive, we'll call you..

83
00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.359
According to the reading, many failures abroad stem from…

84
00:06:27.659 --> 00:06:33.414
A, poor products. B, a lack of cultural competence. C, low tariffs.

85
00:06:36.967 --> 00:06:40.139
The answer is B: a lack of cultural competence.

86
00:06:41.333 --> 00:06:44.473
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:45.317 --> 00:06:49.069
Why is cultural competence important when doing business internationally?

88
00:07:05.883 --> 00:07:07.279
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:07:07.579 --> 00:07:43.081
Cultural competence is important because business relies on trust, and trust depends on understanding how other people communicate and make decisions. When a negotiator misreads politeness as agreement, or ignores the importance of hierarchy, a promising deal can collapse. For example, Cambodian partners may avoid saying 'no' directly, so a foreign manager must learn to recognise indirect signals. In short, cultural competence turns a foreign company into a respected partner.

90
00:07:43.381 --> 00:07:45.172
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:46.083 --> 00:07:50.849
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and when plus clause for conditions.

92
00:07:51.149 --> 00:07:56.297
Today's value is cultural competence. Learn how other cultures work, and treat them with respect.

93
00:07:56.597 --> 00:07:59.099
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:59.399 --> 00:08:02.469
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

